  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (ja): ゲッキツ",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%B2%E3%83%83%E3%82%AD%E3%83%84",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 109581349,
      "text": "ゲッキツ はミカン科ゲッキツ属の常緑小高木の柑橘類。観葉植物としてよく栽培される。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (en): Murraya paniculata",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murraya_paniculata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 1376317348,
      "text": "Murraya paniculata, commonly known as orange jasmine, orange jessamine, china box, cosmetic barktree, or mock orange, is a species of shrub or small tree in the family Rutaceae and is native to South Asia, Southeast Asia and Australia. It has smooth bark, pinnate leaves with up to seven egg-shaped to elliptical leaflets, fragrant white or cream-coloured flowers and oval, orange-red berries containing hairy seeds."
    },
    "es": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (es): Murraya paniculata",
      "source_url": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murraya_paniculata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 171581390,
      "text": "Murraya paniculata es una planta tropical de hojas perennes pequeñas, flores blancas y perfumadas, que se cultiva como un árbol ornamental o de cobertura, originaria del Sureste de Asia desde China a Japón y Malasia. La Murraya está estrechamente relacionada con los cítricos."
    },
    "fr": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Murraya paniculata",
      "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murraya_paniculata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 223980293,
      "text": "Le buis de Chine ou bois jasmin est une espèce d'arbuste sempervirent de sous-bois originaire du Sud-Est asiatique et commun dans de nombreuses régions tropicales. Il est aussi appelé \"bois-jasmin\", \"oranger jasmin\", ou \"bois de satin\"."
    },
    "id": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (id): Kemuning",
      "source_url": "https://id.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kemuning",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 29984721,
      "text": "Kemuning adalah tumbuhan yang berasal dari famili Rutaceae. Perdu yang masih bersaudara dengan daun kari ini memiliki nama daerah seperti; kemuning, kamuning (Sd.), kajeni, kemuning, kemoning (Bl.), kamonèng (Mdr.), kemiuning (Smb.), kamuni (Bm.), sukik (Rt.), kamuning, kamoni (Pmn.), palopo (Bgs.). eseki (Wtr.), tanasa (Aru), kamone (Br.), kamoni (Amb.)"
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Murraya paniculata § Cultivation",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murraya_paniculata#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
        "revision": 1376317348,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Murraya paniculata is cultivated as an ornamental tree or hedge because of its hardiness, wide range of soil tolerance (M. paniculata may grow in alkaline, clayey, sandy, acidic and loamy soils), and is suitable for larger hedges. The plant flowers throughout the year and produces small, fragrant flower clusters which attract bees, while the fruits attract small frugivorous birds.\n\n■ Propagation\nThe orange jessamine is sexually propagated by its seeds. The fruits are eaten by birds, which then pass the seeds out in their feces. It may also be asexually propagated by softwood cuttings.",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "fr": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Murraya paniculata § Culture",
        "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murraya_paniculata#Culture",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
        "revision": 223980293,
        "heading": "Culture",
        "text": "Croissance assez rapide sur les jeunes plants puis plus lente par la suite. Supporte bien la taille mais les boutons floraux se forment plusieurs mois à l’avance, en bout de branches.\nIl s’adapte bien à la culture en intérieur sous climat tempéré, avec maxi à 18 °C l’hiver, mini à 0 °C (courte durée) et une bonne hygrométrie.\nL'espèce est peu sensible aux maladies.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
